==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|acolite}}, {{m|enm|acolit}}, from {{der|en|fro|acolyt}} and {{der|en|LL.|acolythus}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀκόλουθος|t=follower, attendant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæ.kə.laɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-acolyte.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Christianity}} One who has received the highest of the four minor orders in the [[Catholic Church]], being [[ordained]] to carry the [[wine]], [[water]] and [[lights]] at [[Mass]].
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} An [[altar server]].
# An [[attendant]], [[assistant]], or [[follower]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Stephen Collinson|title=New US intel report shows Russia, Trump and GOP acolytes have same goals|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/17/politics/trump-russia-elections/index.html|date=2021-03-17|passage=The real bombshell it contains is not the confidence of the spy agencies that Russia hoped to subvert American democracy. It is that US intelligence experts effectively confirmed that for the second election in a row, Trump '''acolytes''' repeatedly used, knowingly or otherwise, misinformation produced by the spies of one of America’s most sworn foreign adversaries to try to win a US election.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Stephen Collinson|title=Trump rages as former acolytes turn against him under legal heat|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/10/25/politics/trump-former-aides-turn-against-him/index.html|date=2023-10-25|passage=She was the third former Trump '''acolyte''' to agree to testify against the ex-president and others this week.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2025-04-10|author=Adam Serwer|title=The Confrontation Between Trump and the Supreme Court Has Arrived|journal=The Atlantic|titleurl=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2025/04/supreme-court-confronts-donald-trump/682402/|passage=Trump '''acolytes''' have publicly and repeatedly floated the idea of defying court orders with which they disagree.}}

